.\"     @(#)ipconfig.8
.\"
.Dd November 8, 2026
.Dt IPCONFIG 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ipconfig
.Nd view and control IP configuration state
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm waitall
.Op Ar timeout
.Nm
.Cm getifaddr
.Ar interface-name
.Nm
.Cm ifcount
.Nm
.Cm getoption
.Ar interface-name
.Ar (option-name | option-code )
.Nm
.Cm getpacket
.Ar interface-name
.Nm
.Cm getv6packet
.Ar interface-name
.Nm
.Cm getdhcpduid
.Nm
.Cm getsummary
.Ar interface-name
.Nm
.Cm set
.Ar interface-name
.Cm NONE
.Nm
.Cm set
.Ar interface-name
.Cm DHCP
.Nm
.Cm set
.Ar interface-name
.Cm MANUAL
.Ar ip-address
.Ar subnet-mask
.Nm
.Cm set
.Ar interface-name
.Cm NONE-V4
.Nm
.Cm set
.Ar interface-name
.Cm NONE-V6
.Nm
.Cm set
.Ar interface-name
.Cm AUTOMATIC-V6
.Nm
.Cm set
.Ar interface-name
.Cm MANUAL-V6
.Ar ipv6-address
.Ar prefix-length
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a utility that communicates with the IPConfiguration agent
to retrieve and set IP configuration parameters.  It should only be used
in a test and debug context.  Using it for any other purpose is strongly
discouraged.
Public API's in the SystemConfiguration framework are currently
the only supported way to access and control the state of IPConfiguration.
.Pp
The IPConfiguration agent is responsible for configuring and managing the
IP addresses on direct, connectionless interfaces such as IEEE 802.3 Ethernet.
The IPConfiguration agent is the ipconfigd daemon.
.Pp
The IPConfiguration agent implements the client side of the DHCP
protocol described in RFC2131 and RFC2132.  It also
assigns and maintains static IP addresses.
.Pp
In all cases, the IPConfiguration agent performs IP address conflict detection
before assigning an IP address to an interface.
.Sh "COMMANDS"
The
.Nm
utility provides several commands:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm waitall Op Ar timeout
Blocks until all network services have completed configuring, or have timed
out in
the process of configuring, or
.Ar timeout
seconds have passed.  The default is 90 seconds and the maximum is 120.
This is only useful for initial system start-up
time synchronization for legacy network services that are incapable of dealing
with dynamic network configuration changes.
.Pp
.It Cm getifaddr Ar interface-name
Prints to standard output the IPv4 address configured on the interface.
No address is printed, and the exit status is non-zero, if no
service is currently configured or active on the interface.
.Pp
.It Cm ifcount
Prints the number of interfaces that currently have an IPv4 address
configured by the IPConfiguration agent.
.Pp
.It Cm getoption Ar interface-name Ar ( option-name | option-code )
Prints the DHCP option with the given name or option code integer value,
from the packet described under
.Cm getpacket .
Option names are those shown by
.Cm getpacket ,
for example
.Li domain_name
or
.Li router .
An empty
.Ar interface-name
searches all of the interfaces.
If an option has multiple values e.g. \fBdomain_name_server\fR, only the first
value is printed.
.Pp
.It Cm getpacket Ar interface-name
Prints to standard output the DHCP packet that the client accepted from
the DHCP server.  This command is useful to check what the server
provided, and whether the values are sensible.  This command outputs nothing
if DHCP is not active on the interface, or the attempt to acquire an
IP address was unsuccessful.
.Pp
.It Cm getv6packet Ar interface-name
Prints to standard output the DHCPv6 packet that the client last accepted
from a DHCPv6 server.  This command outputs nothing if DHCPv6 is not
active on the interface or no server has answered.
.Pp
.It Cm getdhcpduid
Prints to standard output this host's DHCPv6 client identifier (DUID), for
registering the machine with a DHCPv6 server.
.Pp
.It Cm getsummary Ar interface-name
Prints to standard output a summary of the current IPConfiguration state
over the specific interface.
.Pp
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm NONE
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm DHCP
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm MANUAL Ar ip-address Ar subnet-mask
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm LINKLOCAL
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm BOOTP
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm INFORM Ar ip-address Op Ar subnet-mask
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm FAILOVER Ar ip-address Op Ar subnet-mask Op Ar timeout
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm NONE-V4
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm NONE-V6
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm AUTOMATIC-V6
.It Cm set Ar interface-name Cm MANUAL-V6 Ar ipv6-address Ar prefix-length
Sets the interface to have a new temporary network service of the
given type.  Any existing service of the same address family on the
interface is first de-configured before the new service is instantiated.
The other address family is not changed.
.Pp
If NONE or NONE-V4 is specified, the existing IPv4 service is de-configured.
If NONE-V6 is specified, the existing IPv6 service is de-configured and
IPv6 is turned off on the interface.
.Pp
AUTOMATIC-V6 configures an IPv6 address from Router Advertisements.
.Pp
MANUAL-V6 requires the specification of an IPv6 address
.Ar ipv6-address
and a prefix length
.Ar prefix-length .
A link-local address is not accepted.
.Pp
DHCP requires no additional arguments. The IP address, subnet mask,
router, and DNS information are retrieved automatically.  If no server
responds, a link-local address is configured after about 15 seconds and
DHCP keeps trying; a lease replaces the link-local address.
.Pp
LINKLOCAL configures a 169.254/16 link-local address and requires no
additional arguments.
.Pp
BOOTP requires no additional arguments.  It configures the address from a
BOOTP server, with no lease, and if none responds tries again every two
minutes.
.Pp
INFORM configures the given address and asks a DHCP server for the router,
the DNS servers and, if
.Ar subnet-mask
is not given, the mask.  The address stays if no server responds.
.Pp
FAILOVER configures the given address with no router, starting after up to
five seconds.  If
.Ar timeout
is given and is greater than zero, the address is removed after that many
seconds, until the link or the method changes.
.Pp
MANUAL requires the specification of an IP address
.Ar ip-address
and a subnet mask
.Ar subnet-mask .
.Pp
The \fBset\fR command requires root privileges.
.Pp
\fBNote:\fR The \fBset\fR command is very useful for debugging, but it can't
be used to configure a persistent service.  The temporary service does not
change the saved network configuration and is lost when ipconfigd restarts.
.El
.Sh "EXAMPLES"
.nf
# ipconfig getpacket em0
op = BOOTREPLY
htype = 1
flags = 0
hlen = 6
hops = 0
xid = 1956115059
secs = 0
ciaddr = 0.0.0.0
yiaddr = 192.168.4.10
siaddr = 192.168.4.1
giaddr = 0.0.0.0
chaddr = 0:3:93:7a:d7:5c
sname = dhcp.mycompany.net
file =
options:
Options count is 8
dhcp_message_type (uint8): ACK 0x5
server_identifier (ip): 192.168.4.1
lease_time (uint32): 0x164a
subnet_mask (ip): 255.255.255.0
router (ip_mult): {192.168.4.1}
domain_name_server (ip_mult): {192.168.4.1}
domain_name (string): mycompany.net
end (none):
.fi
.Pp
.nf
# ipconfig getoption em0 router
192.168.4.1
.fi
